	<body>
		<div class="page">
			<header>
				<div class="container nav">
					<a class="brand" href="/">Feranzeey</a>
					<a class="back" href="/blog">← Back to Blog</a>
				</div>
			</header>

			<main>
				<section class="article-header">
					<div class="container">
						<div class="eyebrow">
							DevOps · Kubernetes · Incident Response
						</div>

						<h1>{title}</h1>

						<p class="description">{description}</p>

						<div class="meta">
							Published {date} · Feranzeey
						</div>

						<div class="tags">
							{tags.map((tag) => <span class="tag">{tag}</span>)}
						</div>
					</div>
				</section>

				<article>
					<div class="container">

						<p>
							One of the most interesting problems I explored while
							building PlatformOps AI was not simply how to monitor
							Kubernetes.
						</p>

						<p>
							The harder question was:
						</p>

						<blockquote>
							<strong>
								How can an engineer move from knowing that something
								is broken to understanding what might have caused
								it?
							</strong>
						</blockquote>

						<p>
							Kubernetes can tell us when resources are unhealthy.
							Prometheus can tell us when metrics change. GitHub can
							tell us what changed recently.
						</p>

						<p>
							But an engineer still needs to connect those signals.
						</p>

						<p>
							This became the idea behind the
							<strong>AI-assisted incident investigation</strong>
							workflow in PlatformOps AI.
						</p>

						<h2>The Incident Investigation Problem</h2>

						<p>
							Imagine a production deployment suddenly becomes
							unavailable.
						</p>

						<p>
							The first thing an engineer might see is:
						</p>

						<pre><code>Deployment unavailable
Ready replicas: 0/1</code></pre>

						<p>
							That tells us there is a problem, but it does not
							explain the problem.
						</p>

						<p>
							The engineer may then start checking:
						</p>

						<ul>
							<li>Pod status</li>
							<li>Container restart counts</li>
							<li>Kubernetes events</li>
							<li>Deployment history</li>
							<li>CPU and memory metrics</li>
							<li>Recent application changes</li>
							<li>Recent commits or deployments</li>
						</ul>

						<p>
							This investigation can involve several commands and
							different monitoring tools.
						</p>

						<p>
							The challenge is not necessarily a lack of data.
						</p>

						<p>
							The challenge is connecting the data.
						</p>

						<h2>The Investigation Workflow</h2>

						<p>
							I designed the PlatformOps AI investigation workflow
							around a simple sequence:
						</p>

						<pre class="diagram"><code>Incident Detected
       ↓
Collect Kubernetes State
       ↓
Collect Events
       ↓
Collect Prometheus Metrics
       ↓
Check Recent GitHub Activity
       ↓
Correlate Evidence
       ↓
Investigate Possible Cause
       ↓
Assess Impact
       ↓
Recommend Action
       ↓
Record Investigation</code></pre>

						<p>
							The goal is not to make the system automatically
							declare a root cause without evidence.
						</p>

						<p>
							The goal is to organize the available evidence so an
							engineer can investigate the incident more efficiently.
						</p>

						<h2>Step 1 — Detect the Kubernetes Problem</h2>

						<div class="step">
							<div class="step-number">STEP 01</div>

							<h3>Start with Kubernetes state</h3>

							<p>
								The first layer of the investigation is the
								Kubernetes environment itself.
							</p>

							<p>
								PlatformOps AI can inspect resources such as pods,
								deployments, services, nodes and namespaces.
							</p>
						</div>

						<p>
							For example, a deployment might report:
						</p>

						<pre><code>Deployment: application
Desired replicas: 1
Ready replicas: 0
Available replicas: 0</code></pre>

						<p>
							That gives us the initial signal that something needs
							to be investigated.
						</p>

						<p>
							But we still need more information.
						</p>

						<h2>Step 2 — Investigate the Pods</h2>

						<div class="step">
							<div class="step-number">STEP 02</div>

							<h3>Look underneath the deployment</h3>

							<p>
								A deployment can be unavailable because its pods
								are unhealthy.
							</p>
						</div>

						<p>
							The investigation therefore moves from the deployment
							to the pods running underneath it.
						</p>

						<p>
							For example:
						</p>

						<pre><code>Pod: application-7d8f9c6d7b-x4k2m
Status: CrashLoopBackOff
Restarts: 12</code></pre>

						<p>
							Now the investigation has more context.
						</p>

						<p>
							The problem is no longer simply:
						</p>

						<blockquote>
							Deployment unavailable.
						</blockquote>

						<p>
							We now know that a pod is repeatedly failing.
						</p>

						<h2>Step 3 — Check Kubernetes Events</h2>

						<div class="step">
							<div class="step-number">STEP 03</div>

							<h3>Find what Kubernetes observed</h3>

							<p>
								Kubernetes events can provide additional context
								around resource failures.
							</p>
						</div>

						<p>
							An investigation may encounter events related to:
						</p>

						<ul>
							<li>Failed container starts</li>
							<li>Image pull failures</li>
							<li>Readiness probe failures</li>
							<li>Scheduling problems</li>
							<li>Resource constraints</li>
							<li>Container restarts</li>
						</ul>

						<p>
							For example:
						</p>

						<pre><code>Warning
BackOff

Container failed repeatedly.
Back-off restarting failed container.</code></pre>

						<p>
							This gives the investigation another piece of
							evidence.
						</p>

						<h2>Step 4 — Add Prometheus Metrics</h2>

						<div class="step">
							<div class="step-number">STEP 04</div>

							<h3>Look at system behaviour</h3>

							<p>
								Kubernetes tells us about resource state.
								Prometheus gives us another perspective by
								providing operational metrics.
							</p>
						</div>

						<p>
							PlatformOps AI can use metrics to investigate areas
							such as:
						</p>

						<pre><code>CPU utilization
Memory utilization
Network activity
Container activity
Cluster utilization</code></pre>

						<p>
							For example, suppose the investigation finds:
						</p>

						<pre><code>CPU:
Normal

Memory:
Increasing rapidly

Pod:
Repeatedly restarting</code></pre>

						<p>
							The memory trend becomes another piece of evidence.
						</p>

						<p>
							However, a metric by itself does not prove causation.
						</p>

						<div class="callout">
							<div class="callout-title">
								Important Investigation Principle
							</div>

							<strong>
								A signal is evidence, not automatically a root
								cause.
							</strong>

							<p style="margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 0;">
								This distinction is important when building
								AI-assisted infrastructure tooling.
							</p>
						</div>

						<h2>Step 5 — Check Recent GitHub Activity</h2>

						<div class="step">
							<div class="step-number">STEP 05</div>

							<h3>Ask what changed</h3>

							<p>
								One of the most useful questions during an
								incident is:
							</p>

							<blockquote>
								<strong>What changed recently?</strong>
							</blockquote>
						</div>

						<p>
							PlatformOps AI can use GitHub activity to provide
							additional context around recent changes.
						</p>

						<p>
							For example:
						</p>

						<pre><code>Latest commit:
Update application configuration

Author:
Developer

Recent deployment:
Yes

Deployment became unhealthy:
Shortly afterwards</code></pre>

						<p>
							This does not automatically mean that the commit caused
							the incident.
						</p>

						<p>
							But it provides an important investigation path.
						</p>

						<h2>Step 6 — Correlate the Evidence</h2>

						<p>
							This is where the investigation becomes more
							interesting.
						</p>

						<p>
							Instead of looking at each signal independently, the
							platform can organize the available evidence together.
						</p>

						<pre class="diagram"><code>Kubernetes
    │
    ├── Deployment unavailable
    │
    ├── Pod restarting
    │
    └── Warning event
          │
          ▼
Prometheus
    │
    ├── Memory increasing
    │
    └── CPU normal
          │
          ▼
GitHub
    │
    └── Recent application change
          │
          ▼
    Investigation Context</code></pre>

						<p>
							The purpose of this correlation is to help the
							engineer see the incident as a sequence of related
							signals rather than isolated events.
						</p>

						<h2>Step 7 — AI-Assisted Analysis</h2>

						<p>
							Once the evidence has been collected, AI can help
							structure the investigation.
						</p>

						<p>
							The system can organize the information into areas
							such as:
						</p>

						<pre><code>Issue
Evidence
Possible Cause
Severity
Impact
Recommended Action</code></pre>

						<p>
							For example:
						</p>

						<pre><code>Issue:
Application deployment unavailable

Evidence:
- 0/1 replicas ready
- Pod repeatedly restarting
- Kubernetes BackOff event
- Memory usage increased
- Recent application change detected

Possible Cause:
Application failure following a recent change

Severity:
HIGH

Impact:
Application unavailable

Recommended Action:
Investigate the recent application change
and inspect the failing container logs.</code></pre>

						<p>
							The important wording here is
							<strong>possible cause</strong>.
						</p>

						<p>
							The system should distinguish between what is directly
							observed and what is inferred from the evidence.
						</p>

						<h2>Step 8 — Recommend a Remediation</h2>

						<p>
							After the investigation has been structured, the next
							step is deciding what action should be considered.
						</p>

						<p>
							Depending on the evidence, possible actions might
							include:
						</p>

						<ul>
							<li>Inspecting application logs</li>
							<li>Checking the latest deployment</li>
							<li>Reviewing configuration changes</li>
							<li>Rolling back a problematic deployment</li>
							<li>Restarting a workload when appropriate</li>
							<li>Investigating resource constraints</li>
						</ul>

						<p>
							For example:
						</p>

						<pre><code>kubectl rollout status deployment &lt;deployment-name&gt;

kubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt;

kubectl logs &lt;pod-name&gt;</code></pre>

						<p>
							The platform can provide commands as recommendations
							while keeping execution under appropriate human
							control.
						</p>

						<h2>Step 9 — Record the Incident</h2>

						<p>
							An incident should not disappear from the system just
							because the application eventually becomes healthy.
						</p>

						<p>
							The investigation itself can become useful operational
							knowledge.
						</p>

						<p>
							PlatformOps AI can maintain information around:
						</p>

						<pre><code>Incident
Timestamp
Evidence
Possible Cause
Action
Status
Resolution</code></pre>

						<p>
							This creates a history that can be referenced when
							similar problems occur again.
						</p>

						<h2>Putting Everything Together</h2>

						<p>
							The complete investigation workflow can therefore look
							like this:
						</p>

						<pre class="diagram"><code>                Incident Detected
                       │
                       ▼
              Kubernetes State
                       │
                       ▼
                Pod Investigation
                       │
                       ▼
              Kubernetes Events
                       │
                       ▼
              Prometheus Metrics
                       │
                       ▼
             Recent GitHub Activity
                       │
                       ▼
                Evidence Correlation
                       │
                       ▼
                AI-Assisted Analysis
                       │
                       ▼
              Recommended Action
                       │
                       ▼
                Incident History</code></pre>

						<p>
							This workflow represents the core idea behind
							PlatformOps AI.
						</p>

						<h2>Why This Approach Matters</h2>

						<p>
							Traditional monitoring systems are extremely useful at
							telling engineers that something is wrong.
						</p>

						<p>
							But incident response often requires a second layer of
							work.
						</p>

						<p>
							An engineer has to investigate:
						</p>

						<ul>
							<li>What failed?</li>
							<li>When did it fail?</li>
							<li>What changed?</li>
							<li>What evidence supports the investigation?</li>
							<li>What could explain the behaviour?</li>
							<li>What should be checked next?</li>
						</ul>

						<p>
							That is the gap I wanted PlatformOps AI to explore.
						</p>

						<h2>Monitoring vs Investigation</h2>

						<p>
							One of the biggest lessons from this project was
							understanding that monitoring and investigation are
							different stages of the operational workflow.
						</p>

						<pre><code>Monitoring

Something is wrong
       ↓
Alert


Investigation

Something is wrong
       ↓
Collect evidence
       ↓
Understand context
       ↓
Investigate possible causes
       ↓
Determine next action</code></pre>

						<p>
							The first workflow tells us that attention is needed.
						</p>

						<p>
							The second helps us understand what to do next.
						</p>

						<h2>What I Learned Building This</h2>

						<h3>1. Context is extremely important</h3>

						<p>
							A single Kubernetes status or metric rarely provides
							the complete picture.
						</p>

						<p>
							Combining multiple operational signals provides a
							richer investigation context.
						</p>

						<h3>2. Correlation is harder than collection</h3>

						<p>
							Collecting metrics is relatively straightforward.
							The more difficult problem is determining which signals
							are actually relevant to an incident.
						</p>

						<h3>3. AI should support engineers</h3>

						<p>
							I designed the AI component as an investigation
							assistant rather than a replacement for engineering
							judgement.
						</p>

						<p>
							The underlying infrastructure evidence should remain
							visible.
						</p>

						<h3>4. Recommendations need context</h3>

						<p>
							A command is more useful when an engineer understands
							why it is being recommended.
						</p>

						<h3>5. Human control still matters</h3>

						<p>
							Infrastructure actions can have consequences.
							Therefore, automated recommendations and automatic
							execution should be treated as separate capabilities.
						</p>

						<h2>What I Would Improve Next</h2>

						<p>
							There are several areas I would continue exploring.
						</p>

						<ul>
							<li>
								More sophisticated incident correlation
							</li>
							<li>
								Better historical incident comparison
							</li>
							<li>
								More Kubernetes resource coverage
							</li>
							<li>
								Richer log analysis
							</li>
							<li>
								Improved deployment-change correlation
							</li>
							<li>
								Safer remediation workflows
							</li>
							<li>
								More detailed incident reports
							</li>
						</ul>

						<p>
							I would also continue improving the distinction between
							observed evidence, inferred causes and recommended
							actions.
						</p>

						<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>

						<p>
							Building PlatformOps AI changed the way I think about
							infrastructure monitoring.
						</p>

						<p>
							The challenge isn't only collecting more data.
						</p>

						<p>
							The challenge is making the available information
							useful during an incident.
						</p>

						<blockquote>
							<strong>
								Good incident response starts with good evidence.
							</strong>
						</blockquote>

						<p>
							Kubernetes provides the infrastructure state.
							Prometheus provides operational metrics. GitHub provides
							development activity.
						</p>

						<p>
							PlatformOps AI brings these signals together and uses
							AI-assisted analysis to help structure the
							investigation.
						</p>

						<p>
							That is the direction I wanted to explore with this
							project:
						</p>

						<blockquote>
							<strong>
								From monitoring what is happening to helping
								engineers understand why it might be happening.
							</strong>
						</blockquote>

						<h2>Explore PlatformOps AI</h2>

						<p>
							The complete project is available on GitHub.
						</p>

						<a
							class="project-link"
							href="https://github.com/feranzeey/platformops-ai"
							target="_blank"
							rel="noopener noreferrer"
						>
							View PlatformOps AI on GitHub →
						</a>

